    "created_at": "2016-08-09T11:08:59Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-08-09T11:08:59Z",
    "body": "No, in most cases it should return a number even if there's trailing garbage (e.g. `coords`). `input` has \n\n> The value sanitization algorithm is as follows: If the value of the element is not a valid floating-point number, then set it to the empty string instead.\n\nSo \"valid floating-point number\" is sometimes used by UA algorithms, but typically it's about document conformance and the algorithm uses \"rules for parsing floating-point number values\".\n\nWe could add a note that explains this, or we could add \"strict\" parsing modes that error on trailing garbage (or both).\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
